<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pillar of Eliseg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pillar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisedd-ap-gwylog">Eliseg</a> is a stone cross shaft near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, raised in the 9th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyngen-ap-cadell">Cyngen ap Cadell</a>, king of Powys, in honour of his great-grandfather Eliseg. Its now largely illegible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription traced the royal line of Powys back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> and the Roman usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a>, making it an important source for early Welsh kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg.jpg" alt="Pillar of Eliseg" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elisegs_Pfeiler_5_S%C3%BCdost.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The pillar stands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valle-crucis-abbey">Valle Crucis Abbey</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>, within the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a>, on a large artificial mound far older than itself. Its builder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyngen-ap-cadell">Cyngen ap Cadell</a>, died in 855, and the man it honours is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisedd-ap-gwylog">Elisedd ap Gwylog</a>; the spelling Eliseg on the stone is thought to be a mistake of the carver. Excavations by Project Eliseg in 2010, 2011 and 2012 showed that the mound began as a kerbed platform cairn of around 2000 BCE and was raised in a second phase, with cists holding burnt human bone and 7 kg of cremated bone from adults, juveniles and infants, a flint knife and a bone pin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscription ran to some thirty-one lines of insular script, one of the longest surviving from pre-Viking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. It traces Cyngen's descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisedd-ap-gwylog">Eliseg</a> through Brochmail and Cattell and says that Eliseg joined together the inheritance of Powys, winning it from the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> with his sword and with fire. It asks whoever reads it to bless the soul of Eliseg. Later lines, now broken, name Maximus, and Britu son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a>, whom Germanus blessed and whom Sevira, daughter of Maximus, bore to him. It closes by naming Conmarch, who painted the writing at the request of King Cyngen, and by blessing the king, his household and all Powys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The inscription names several people found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> and adds to what that text records. The pillar was thrown down by the Roundheads during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, and a grave beneath it was opened. The antiquarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-lhuyd">Edward Lhuyd</a> examined it and copied the inscription in 1696; Robert Vermaat judges his transcript to have been remarkably accurate. In 1773 the landowner, Trevor Lloyd, is said to have opened a stone cist and removed a skeleton and artefacts. The lower half of the pillar was lost, but the upper half was set up again in 1779.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pillar of Eliseg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The inscription on the <b>Pillar of Eliseg</b> is now almost illegible; its text is known mainly because the scholar Edward Lhuyd copied it in 1696.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
